Files distributed with MSstatsConvertLLM
========================================

AlphaPept_subset.csv
AlphaPept_annotation.csv
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

AlphaPept output for the example in the vignette "1. Converting the output
of a tool without a converter". MSstatsConvert has no AlphaPept converter, so
the vignette converts this file with a conversion specification proposed by a
language model.

Source
  ProteoBench, file test/data/quant/quant_lfq_ion_DDA_QExactive/
  AlphaPept_subset.csv.
  https://github.com/Proteobench/ProteoBench
  Retrieved 27 October 2026.

License
  The ProteoBench repository, including this file, is distributed under the
  Apache License 2.0. https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0

Underlying measurements
  A Q Exactive HF-X DDA subset of the mixed-species LFQ benchmark of

    Van Puyvelde, B. et al. A comprehensive LFQ benchmark dataset on modern
    day acquisition strategies in proteomics. Scientific Data 9, 126 (2022).
    https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-022-01216-6

  deposited in PRIDE as PXD028735. The article is open access under CC BY 4.0.

Experimental design
  Human, yeast and E. coli digests are mixed in two proportions, conditions A
  and B. ProteoBench uses the first biological replicate series, "alpha",
  with three technical replicates of each condition, six runs in all. Human is
  present in equal amounts in both conditions, yeast is twice as abundant in
  A, and E. coli is four times as abundant in B, so the expected log2 fold
  changes of B against A are 0, -1 and +2.

  AlphaPept_annotation.csv lists the six runs. Each run has its own
  BioReplicate, so MSstats compares A with B across the three runs of each
  condition, as ProteoBench does. The BioReplicate labels include the
  condition, since a BioReplicate that appears in both conditions would make
  MSstats treat the design as paired.

Subset
  To keep the package small, the subset contains every row of a protein seen
  in at least four of the six runs, and every row flagged as a decoy, which a
  filter-aware prompt should find. This leaves 7,106 of the 9,814 rows, 462
  proteins and all 44 decoy rows, in 851 kB. The columns are unchanged.

  Rebuild with: Rscript inst/scripts/make_alphapept_data.R

alphapept_specification.rds
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The conversion specification that deepseek-r1-14b returned for
AlphaPept_subset.csv under the filter_aware prompt. It was obtained once, with
Ollama on a local computer, and is distributed so that the vignette runs
without a language model.

  Rebuild with: Rscript inst/scripts/make_alphapept_specification.R

pd_case_study.rds
spectronaut_case_study.rds
metamorpheus_case_study.rds
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The saved data read by the vignettes "2. Checking the intensity column"
(Proteome Discoverer, PXD005642), "3. Checking the filters" (Spectronaut,
Puyvelde2022) and "4. Checking the peptide sequence" (MetaMorpheus,
Solivais2024). Each file is a list with

  rows           Rows of the export, with the column names the tool wrote.
                 The first three are the rows the model was shown. For
                 Spectronaut, the rows are the fragments of the first
                 precursor.
  forms          MetaMorpheus only: the first peptide that the export reports
                 both with and without a modification, in one run, at one
                 charge and in one protein group.
  specification  The conversion specification of the saved benchmark trial
                 that the downstream comparison used, repeat 1 of
                 llama3.1-8b with the constrained_v3 prompt (Proteome
                 Discoverer, Spectronaut) or the lean prompt (MetaMorpheus).
  results        The case's panel of the downstream figure, from
                 downstream_panels.rds: one row per protein and comparison,
                 the hand-written converter in the _h columns and the
                 LLM-based converter in the _l columns.

The rows come from the deposited datasets of the manuscript, truncated to
their first rows, in benchmark_data/ of the GitHub repository. The
specifications and the results are cut from the saved trials and
downstream_panels.rds below, so nothing is recomputed.

  Rebuild with: Rscript inst/scripts/make_case_study_data.R

raw_trials_benchmark_20261007_163702.rds
downstream_panels.rds
benchmark_columns.rds
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The saved results of the benchmark in the manuscript: the 288 benchmark
trials, the per-protein adjusted p-values of the differential abundance
comparisons in the manuscript, and the column names of each public dataset.
paper/README.md in the GitHub repository reproduces Table 2 and the
downstream figure from them, and inst/scripts/make_case_study_data.R cuts the
case-study files above from the first two. They were produced from the runs
in results/ by inst/scripts/make_benchmark_data.R.
